      "narrative": "fab2, a Seed-stage semiconductor company founded by Jim Keller and Sam Zeloof, has raised $15M+ to date. The valuation analysis draws from a peer universe of 8 private and 3 public semiconductor companies, including Series A-stage comparables SambaNova Systems ($11.0B), Rebellions ($2.3B), and d-Matrix ($2.0B).\n\nTwo valuation methods triangulate a defensible range. The Stage-Progression method applies a 2.5x multiplier typical of Seed-to-next-stage advancement to the $15M raised proxy, implying $38M. The Peer-Median method anchors to three same-sector, same/adjacent-stage private companies, yielding a $2.3B median with a range spanning $2.0B to $11.0B. Public-comp anchoring via P/S multiples (20.5x median across ALAB, NVDA, AMD) cannot be applied without disclosed revenue figures. The stage-progression method is conservative but grounded in capital progression; peer medians reflect actual market pricing but exhibit wide dispersion, particularly the $11.0B outlier from SambaNova.\n\nThe two applicable methods produce a triangulated range of $32M (low) to $2.7B (high), with a median of $1.2B. Key uncertainties include the appropriateness of Series A peer comparables for a Seed-stage company and the significant valuation gap between the stage-progression result and peer-derived estimates, suggesting either that fab2's risk profile or technology defensibility materially differs from the peer set, or that early-stage semiconductor ventures command meaningful stage discounts relative to later funding rounds.",
