      "narrative": "AIM Intelligent Machines, founded by Adam Sadilek, is a Series A robotics and manufacturing company that has raised $50M to date. Valuation analysis draws on a peer set of nine companies across the sector\u2014one public and eight private\u2014including recent comparables such as Generalist AI, Bedrock Robotics, Dexterity, and Walden Robotics at Series B and Seed stages.\n\nTwo triangulation methods are applicable. Stage-progression analysis applies a 2.0x step-up multiplier typical of Series A advancement to the $50M raised proxy, implying a valuation of $100M. Peer-median analysis across four same-sector, same/adjacent-stage comparables yields a median valuation of $1.7B, with a range from $1.1B to $2.0B. The dispersion reflects mixed staging within the peer cohort, with three Series B companies clustered at $1.6B\u2013$2.0B and one Seed-stage company at $1.1B.\n\nThe triangulated analysis supports a valuation range of $85M to $2.0B, with a median of $900M. The substantial gap between stage-progression and peer-median methods reflects tension between AIM's early-stage capital deployment relative to later-stage peer valuations. Key uncertainties include the maturity of AIM's core technology, revenue traction relative to peers, and whether peer valuations appropriately reflect sector dynamics. A refined estimate would benefit from traction-based metrics and revenue multiples.",
