      "narrative": "Electric Air is a Seed-stage climate and energy company founded by Chris Mui and Jeremy Osbourne that has raised $500K to date. The valuation analysis draws from a comp universe of nine peers in Climate & Energy: one public company (Linz, LNZA) and eight private firms including Redwood Materials, QuantumScape, KoBold Metals, Electric Hydrogen, and Enovix. However, the absence of same-stage or adjacent-stage comparables within the narrow peer set precluded a reliable peer-median valuation.\n\nThe analysis relies primarily on stage-progression methodology, which applies a typical 2.5x step-up multiplier from Seed to the next financing stage, using the $500K total raised as a valuation proxy. This yields an implied value of $1.25M. A triangulated range incorporating conservative and optimistic variants produces estimates of $1.06M (low), $1.25M (median), and $1.44M (high), all converging near the $1M band.\n\nThe single-method foundation creates material uncertainty. The valuation is highly sensitive to the appropriateness of the 2.5x multiplier for Electric Air's specific risk profile, technology maturity, and capital efficiency relative to sector norms. Absent comparable transactions at the same stage, market validation through investor signals or revenue traction would materially refine this estimate. The Climate & Energy sector's heterogeneity\u2014spanning hardware, materials, and software\u2014further limits the precision of stage-based extrapolation alone.",
